@page
@model CaseStudyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Case Study";
}

<section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Case study</p>
        <h1>Enterprise Insurance ERP — Non-Motor Claims</h1>
        <p class="page-lead">Backend contributions on a claims module used by Saudi clients, within a large insurance ERP undergoing modernization toward an API-based architecture on .NET 10 / ASP.NET Core 10.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="shell narrow prose">
        <h2>Context</h2>
        <p>
            The system is a large-scale enterprise insurance ERP serving the Saudi Arabian market.
            I work in the Non-Motor Claims department as a .NET Full Stack Developer at Acess Meditech Private Limited,
            with the strongest focus on backend APIs, service-layer logic and SQL Server.
        </p>
        <p>
            The application is undergoing legacy modernization toward .NET 10 / ASP.NET Core 10.
            That migration is ongoing — not every production module runs on .NET 10 today.
            My work is development and maintenance inside that modernization effort, not a solo redesign of the entire platform.
        </p>

        <h2>Problem areas I touch</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Claims workflows: estimation, reserve validation and settlement</li>
            <li>Dashboard responsiveness when large volumes of records are loaded</li>
            <li>Payment-term processing that spans related modules such as Reinsurance (RI)</li>
            <li>Scheduled background processing, documents, notifications and production reports</li>
            <li>Authority-matrix rules for business authorization and approvals</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Dashboard performance</h2>
        <p>
            The claims dashboard can slow down when handling large volumes of records.
            I contributed to improving responsiveness by optimizing the DataTables configuration and implementing
            pagination and asynchronous data loading, so data is fetched and rendered in smaller pages.
            This was a practical performance improvement on an existing screen — not a full dashboard redesign —
            and no invented response-time metrics are claimed here.
        </p>

        <h2>Cross-module payment processing</h2>
        <p>
            Payment-term workflows interact with other modules, including Reinsurance (RI).
            I implemented transaction-scoped processing with <code>TransactionScope</code> so related updates
            within the scope are committed or rolled back together, reducing the risk of partial updates.
            This describes transactional consistency for the operations enclosed in that scope.
            It does not claim that every operation across independent systems is guaranteed to be atomic.
        </p>

        <h2>Other backend responsibilities</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>REST APIs and service-layer business logic for claims flows</li>
            <li>SQL Server stored procedures, triggers, indexing and query troubleshooting</li>
            <li>Quartz-based scheduled jobs, Windows services and SQL Server Agent jobs for timed processing</li>
            <li>Document upload and download, plus email and SMS notification services</li>
            <li>Production reports for Non-Motor Claims</li>
            <li>External middleware API integration and production support debugging</li>
            <li>Authority-matrix business authorization rules</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Stack in this project</h2>
        <p>
            C#, .NET / ASP.NET Core, REST APIs, Microsoft SQL Server, TransactionScope,
            Quartz-based jobs, Windows services, Visual Studio, TFS, Postman,
            with working knowledge of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, jQuery, Knockout.js and DataTables on the UI side.
        </p>

        <p class="inline-links">
            <a asp-page="/Experience">See the full experience list →</a>
            <a asp-page="/Contact">Discuss a role →</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
